---
import Legal from '../../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';
const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What the integration does' },
  { id: 'requirements', label: 'What you need' },
  { id: 'connect', label: 'How to connect' },
  { id: 'disconnect', label: 'How to disconnect' },
  { id: 'permissions', label: 'Permissions' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'How your data is handled' },
];
---

<Legal
  title="Instagram DM integration"
  description={`How ${site.name} connects to an Instagram professional account to handle direct messages, which permissions it asks for and why, and how to disconnect.`}
  intro={`Connect an Instagram professional account so its direct messages arrive in your ${site.name} inbox, next to your WhatsApp, Messenger and Telegram conversations.`}
  eyebrow="Integrations · Messaging channel"
  badge="Live"
  toc={toc}
  dated={false}
>
  <h2 id="what">What the integration does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Shows direct messages sent to the account you connect in your {site.name} inbox.</li>
    <li>Sends the replies that you, your teammates or the AI write back through Instagram.</li>
    <li>Lets the AI answer from your knowledge base and hand a conversation to your team.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    {site.name}’s messaging features never post to your account, and {site.name} does not access accounts you did not connect. If you use
    Social Studio to publish posts, nothing is published until a person on your team approves it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An Instagram professional account (business or creator). Personal accounts cannot be connected.</li>
    <li>A Facebook Page linked to that Instagram account, which you can manage.</li>
    <li>Permission to manage that account’s messages.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="connect">How to connect</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Sign in to {site.name} at <a href={site.appUrl}>app.chithii.com</a> and open <strong>Settings → Messaging channels</strong> in your project.</li>
    <li>Choose to connect Instagram. You are taken to Meta to log in.</li>
    <li>Review the permissions shown and choose the account to connect.</li>
    <li>Return to {site.name}. New direct messages start appearing in your inbox.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="disconnect">How to disconnect or revoke access</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>In {site.name}:</strong> open Settings → Messaging channels in your project and disconnect the account. New messages stop arriving straight away.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>In Instagram or Facebook:</strong> open the settings that list apps and websites or business integrations connected to your
      account, find {site.name} and remove it. This revokes the access you granted, even if you can no longer sign in to {site.name}.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>What happens to messages already stored after you disconnect is described on the <a href="/data-deletion">data deletion page</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="permissions">Permissions {site.name} asks for</h2>
  <p>{site.name} uses Facebook Login. Instagram accounts are connected through the Facebook Page they are linked to.</p>
  <table>
    <caption class="sr-only">Permissions {site.name} requests</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">Why {site.name} needs it</th><th scope="col">What it allows</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>instagram_basic</code></td><td>To know which Instagram professional account you connected.</td><td>Reading the account’s basic profile information, such as its username.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>instagram_manage_messages</code></td><td>To show direct messages sent to your account and send the replies you or the AI write.</td><td>Reading and sending Instagram direct messages for that account.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="data">How your data is handled</h2>
  <p>
    {site.name} uses the messages, usernames and attachments from your connected account only to show conversations to your team, to generate
    suggested or automatic replies, and to send replies back. The <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> explains what is stored, who processes
    it and for how long.
  </p>
</Legal>
